    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d72">

                <pb facs="0197" n="107" sameAs="frus:frus1875v01/pg107"/>

                <head>No. 68.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Partridge to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Rio de
                                Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1875-02-21"><hi rendition="#italic">February</hi> 21, 1875</date>.
                        (Received March 23.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 234.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: It would appear from the accounts we have
                    from Buenos Ayres that, as soon as the late revolution there was suppressed,
                    that government has again turned its attention to the arming and fortifying of
                    the island of Martin Garcia.</p>
                <p>This fact causes uneasiness here; and, if this design is persisted in, it will
                    certainly cause unfriendly feelings, and possibly hostility, on the part of
                    Brazil.</p>
                <p>In communications from this legation numbered 147 and 148,<anchor n="*"
                        xml:id="d72-f1"/> this matter has already been explained to the Department;
                    and the continuation of military works on, or occupation of that island by the
                    Argentines can only mean that the party now in power there mean to provoke
                    hostilities with this country.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">* * * * * * *</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JAMES R. PARTRIDGE.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
